%N A401254 Primitive anti-abundant numbers: anti-abundant numbers whose anti-divisors are all anti-deficient or anti-perfect.
%C A401254 An integer k is anti-abundant if the sum of its anti-divisors is greater than k (A192268). Here, an anti-abundant number k is called primitive if no anti-divisor of k is anti-abundant.
%C A401254 Unlike standard primitive abundant numbers (A006036), where the condition applies to ordinary divisors, this sequence applies the minimality constraint directly to the set of anti-divisors.
%C A401254 Since 7 is anti-abundant, 7 cannot be an anti-divisor of any term k > 7. Because 7 is an anti-divisor of k > 7 if and only if 2*k == -1 or 1 (mod 7), no term k > 7 satisfies k == 3 or k == 4 (mod 7).
%C A401254 Similarly, since 10 is anti-abundant and 2*m is an anti-divisor of k if and only if k is an odd multiple of m with k > m, no term can be an odd multiple of 5. In particular, no term ends in the digit 5.
%C A401254 For 14 < k <= 10^6, every term except 569716 ends in the digit 2, 3, 7, or 8. All odd terms discovered up to 100000 are prime numbers.
%e A401254 For k = 12, the anti-divisors are 5 and 8. The sum of anti-divisors is 5 + 8 = 13 > 12, so 12 is anti-abundant. The anti-divisors of 5 are {2, 3} with sum 5 <= 5; the anti-divisors of 8 are {3, 5} with sum 8 <= 8. Neither 5 nor 8 is anti-abundant, so 12 is in the sequence.
%e A401254 For k = 197, 197 is prime with 5 anti-divisors 2, 3, 5, 79, 131 summing to 220 > 197, and none of its anti-divisors is anti-abundant.
%o A401254 (Python)
%o A401254 def is_anti_abundant(n):
%o A401254     s = 0
%o A401254     for m in range(1, n // 3 + 1):
%o A401254         if n % m == 0 and (n // m) % 2 == 1:
%o A401254             s += 2 * m
%o A401254     for val in (2 * n - 1, 2 * n + 1):
%o A401254         d = 3
%o A401254         while d * d <= val:
%o A401254             if val % d == 0:
%o A401254                 if 1 < d < n:
%o A401254                     s += d
%o A401254                 q = val // d
%o A401254                 if q != d and 1 < q < n:
%o A401254                     s += q
%o A401254             d += 2
%o A401254     return s > n
%o A401254 def anti_divisors(n):
%o A401254     ads = []
%o A401254     for m in range(1, n // 3 + 1):
%o A401254         if n % m == 0 and (n // m) % 2 == 1:
%o A401254             ads.append(2 * m)
%o A401254     for val in (2 * n - 1, 2 * n + 1):
%o A401254         d = 3
%o A401254         while d * d <= val:
%o A401254             if val % d == 0:
%o A401254                 if 1 < d < n:
%o A401254                     ads.append(d)
%o A401254                 q = val // d
%o A401254                 if q != d and 1 < q < n:
%o A401254                     ads.append(q)
%o A401254             d += 2
%o A401254     return ads
%o A401254 def generate_terms(limit):
%o A401254     is_aa = [False] * (limit + 1)
%o A401254     for k in range(1, limit + 1):
%o A401254         is_aa[k] = is_anti_abundant(k)
%o A401254     res = []
%o A401254     for k in range(1, limit + 1):
%o A401254         if is_aa[k]:
%o A401254             if not any(is_aa[d] for d in anti_divisors(k)):
%o A401254                 res.append(k)
%o A401254     return res
